      "remediation": "Revoke and rotate the exposed key immediately in the payment provider's dashboard. Remove the literal from source and load it from a secrets manager or environment variable (e.g. `paymentApiKey: process.env.PAYMENT_API_KEY`) with a startup check that fails closed if unset. Add the secret to a `.gitignore`-backed/separate secret store and purge it from git history (e.g. git-filter-repo/BFG) since it remains recoverable in prior commits."
    },
    {
      "category": "open-redirect",
      "severity": "MEDIUM",
      "files": ["src/redirect.js"],
      "evidence": "`continueTo` calls `res.redirect(req.query.next)` with no validation, allowlist, or same-origin check on the `next` query parameter. An attacker can craft `GET /continue?next=https://evil.example/steal` so that victims who follow the trusted link are transparently bounced to an attacker-controlled host. This enables phishing campaigns that abuse the trusted domain, and can be chained with token-in-URL flows to leak authorization codes/tokens to the attacker via the redirect.",
      "remediation": "Do not redirect to arbitrary absolute URLs. Validate `req.query.next` against an allowlist of permitted relative paths, or verify it is a server-relative path starting with `/` and not `//`/`/\\`. On any validation failure, redirect to a safe default (e.g. `/`) or return 400. Reject schemes such as `http:`, `https:`, and protocol-relative URLs."
    }
  ],
  "scanned_files": [
    "src/config.js",
    "src/health.js",
    "src/redirect.js"
